<b>Interview 14. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, landscape designer, male, born in 1970s, Beijing</b>
 
<p><b>Do you think that Fairytale is the interpretation of China’s current situation?</b>
<br>Fairytale is completed through a group of people. After the performance, these people returned to various areas of China. Hence the influence, including their understanding of Germany, would spread widely. For example, one participant would pass his information to dozens of people, and thus one man expands into dozens. I feel that it is more difficult than to complete a simple artist work, and certainly more meaningful.  The work by &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, I think that is very meaningful in terms of its implication.  And the implication keeps growing more meaningful.  Marcel Duchamp made a urinal into a work. You say it is art, so it is art.  Since Marcel Duchamp, modern art to a large part has become a matter of concept. The way you attempt to share an idea to others, and this idea is able to influence others, this could be art.
<p>In fact I somewhat simply join in the fun, and indeed like many others, could not understand. The exhibit, the conditions in the hall including the temperature is very warm, and humid, so I showed up, walked around, and then walked out. To be sure, I am not familiar with Chinese arts, but I got the sense that foreigners’ understanding of modern art is relatively advanced and accurate. That is why so many people-Europeans, from so many countries all over the world, are interested in the exhibition, including &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;’s work. Of course, there is a Chinese background and the avant-garde of the work itself. The so-called avant-garde, I think is more about the expression of your inner heart. What is truly expressed is your own thing.

<p><b>You just mentioned inner heart, so what do you think is the relationship between the inner heart of &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and his work?</b>
<br>It is related. This is his understanding of art. He attempted to use this way to express his understanding, and its influence on himself.  I think that it is an expression of inner heart.
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; tried to express this with many aspects, and what he has done seems very complicated, however, it all in fact has something to do with his thinking. I am not especially familiar with him, he is very concerned with many things including the living situation in China, the status of Chinese people in the world, through the way of irony, laughter, anger, and scolding.

<p><b>How do see these chairs in the Exhibition Hall?</b>
<br>They have a very strong  message. They are being placed in all the exhibition halls, and enable visitors to feel their existence everywhere. It feels like the Chinese in Kassel are now, everywhere.  Is this sign good or bad, it’s up to people. I saw many visitors, being seated, perfectly as part of the exhibition, and to very good effect. The sign I understand is not necessarily modern. Each of us now is dressed very internationally, but nobody would comment on us if we still wear Chinese dresses or what. First of all, are we very modern? But these chairs, and other fixed items, I think are of certain Chinese signs and color, including something lost in China’s modernization process. So they may represent nothing but China, neither China’s modernity or precedentedness.

<p><b>Could you see the gap between China and Germany through this exhibit?</b>
<br>There is a big gap between China and Germany. For example in the 798 area*, what we see are artists or art fans who are dressed strangely or with strange hairstyles such as their head shaved. But what we see in Kassel are extremely ordinary people, including old men and women who enjoyed some avant-garde works very much. I think that is a huge gap between the two cultures.  Some Chinese seniors may not understand what is the point of modern art. A pile of messy stuff, and why do it? So it is a huge gap. You do not understand, and you might reject  it, but it might also stimulate your desire to understand it. You try to understand it, understand its background, and if an art work can realize such an effect, it is pretty enough.

<p><b>A work needs to cause an emotional reaction from people to be effective?</b>
<br>I always think that as a good artist, his/her influence not only cause some fluctuations, but also stimulate something relatively noble, not necessary noble, more about a kind of positiveness. Only a little positive, may be better. Of course many arts may lead to something negative, that is another matter. From my point of view, if an artist work can create something positive, it would be better.

<p><b>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;has a particularly strong ability to be involved in self-expression, do you think that he is the kind of person who builds his own system?</b>
<br>He has the sensibility of an artist, and he is involved in many fields, while he keeps doing and expressing  what he likes, and expressing himself not badly. All these things put together, might be gradually interpreted by people, and create the so-called system.

<p><b>You are working on scenic design, what is your impression of German architecture?</b>
<br>We working on architecture or landscape design always holds this opinion, that is, where your roots are located, no matter how much things are modern including foreign messages, what you really think and do, including those very avant-garde or fashionable, it is still Chinese. So right now I care more about inner things. For example, many scenes and architecture we saw in Kassel, are very German, the steel being very thin.  I do not think that Kassel has many famous architects, but the buildings I saw are of great German features. They usually are not very big, but from first sight we know they were designed by German architects. First of all is their accuracy, including that of design and of construction. This cannot be seen in other countries. As to Chinese architecture, most people now are copying formats, and this is very sad.  Maybe a dozen or two have already copied foreign ideas or had their own ideas, which makes me feel such hope.

<p><b>Do you think that plagiarism has much to do with education?</b>
<br>Yes I think it does a lot.  I think the a lot of people are still obsessed with this education system, and even feel that it is very good. Sure, occasionally some come out to attack it, but they never begin with themselves. Since you have recognized that the education system is like this, have you educated yourselves to detach from this system?  People can change, and it is not the case that you shall not remain the same fashion because that teacher has taught you for twenty years. You have another forty, fifty years or even sixty years.

<p><b>What has education given to you?</b>
<br>Education has given me a lot, including many methods, some methods to do concrete things. Of course, there is the transition of knowledge, but I think it is more about methods, methods to do concrete things. Of course there are some methods that you have to explore yourself, incorporate and borrow, including how to copy from others, that we mentioned a moment ago. This is very important.

<p><b>Do you have a sense of insecurity?</b>
<br>Currently I am very much lacking in security. Previously I had a period of relative confidence in when I did well, and avant-garde, with my own way.  My way of thinking changed after having a baby. How can I stay true to my origins without entirely returning to my former self and old ways? How to obtain your own methods? How to deal with the dilemma? Faced with the turbulent wave of construction, how can you find your own position? How to make something useful?  By "useful" I mean domestic Chinese people find it very useful.

<p><b>I would like to ask about your opinion on family or love.</b>
<br>I am of an ethnic minority, and I have a strong sense of family. My family is outstanding, and I am so grateful to them, including children, and the support from my family, the love from them. What I give to them is relatively small, because a lot of my job and things are for society. Family is the fundamental unit of society, and it makes you relatively stable when dealing with many things in many aspects, and make you devote yourself to the cause with more energy.

<p><b>Do you have any faith?</b>
<br>I do not have a faith.  How to address it?  I admire people with faith a lot.  Faith is also a kind of balance, a sense of spiritual peace. People without faith, when they are deprived, have no place to express, or no people to talk with. Even with their lovers, they sometimes cannot talk. It is good to have a faith, I think.

<p>*Translator's note: 798 is an artist area in Beijing

